---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

// Nova DAX and Skippy DAX; `status` omitted = live description shown.
// Meta DAX is written out directly below, first in the list, because it is
// the one that is live.
const initiatives = [
  { name: 'Nova DAX', status: 'Coming soon' },
  { name: 'Skippy DAX', status: 'Coming soon' },
];

// daxfoundation.org home, rebuilt 2026-09-29 at Jason's request:
//
//   1. The DAX line, alone, as it always was.
//   2. The Constraint Protocol as the featured section: the ground everything
//      stands on, one picture, and the way out to the specification.
//   3. Initiatives. Meta DAX is short on purpose -- one line of what it is,
//      the benchmark, one picture, and a link to the repository, which is
//      where everything about Meta DAX is read now (the long #learning copy
//      that lived here moved into the metadax README the same day).
//   4. The definitions moved to their own page, /definitions/. Old links to
//      daxfoundation.org/#cognitive-companion and the rest still land: the
//      inline script at the foot of this page forwards any definition anchor
//      to /definitions/ with the same anchor.
//   5. Reading: everything published on jeyanandan.com, in series order, plus
//      the Fuckery framework itself.
//   6. The note from the founder, with his personal Ko-fi. The disclosure
//      sentence beside that link is a deploy gate: the Foundation is not
//      incorporated and takes no money; what is given there goes to Jason.
//      2026-10-01: the note is about Meta DAX, so it moved into the Meta DAX
//      row (v2 text, Jason's), directly under the repository link, and the
//      standalone section at the foot is gone. It keeps id="founder" so the
//      old #founder links still land.
//   7. 2026-10-01: the Meta DAX row also says how the small experiments earn
//      the big ones (the bridge figure), and carries the human delta value
//      thread, which Jason calls "almost the point": invert the ratio of
//      students to teachers, hand teachers their cognition back on purpose.
//
// Accuracy rules that bind every word here (state/initiatives/
// content-publishing.md): never describe the Foundation as a non-profit,
// charity, registered or incorporated body; the Constraint Protocol has run in
// full once -- say so, and never imply it runs across anyone's traffic today;
// Meta DAX results come from automated runs, and the repository says which.
//
// Figures are self-hosted under /figures/ and drawn in this site's palette
// (no accent colour). Their sources are generated; see figures.py in the
// state/staging/daxfoundation-0929/ folder that shipped them.
//
// Comments in the markup below are {/* ... */}, never <!-- ... -->.

// Every id that used to live on this page and now lives on /definitions/.
const moved = [
  'definitions', 'nonbiological-intelligence', 'biological-intelligence',
  'principal', 'delegate', 'constraint', 'invariant-constraint',
  'dax-constraint', 'cognitive-stack', 'knowledge', 'intelligence', 'wisdom',
  'consciousness', 'emergence', 'top-down-causation', 'constraint-resonance',
  'cognitive-companion', 'human-delta-value', 'ai-as-substrate',
];
---
<Base>
  <section class="lede" aria-labelledby="dax">
    <h1 id="dax">DAX: preservation and expansion of life, humanity, and consciousness.</h1>
  </section>

  <section class="block feature" aria-labelledby="cp">
    <h2 id="cp">Constraint Protocol</h2>
    <p class="big">The ground everything here stands on.</p>
    <p class="body">
      A way for parties &mdash; people, companies or software &mdash; to declare
      in advance what they will not do, and then be checked against it. It keeps
      a signed record nobody can quietly edit, keeps witnessing and judging
      separate, lets a declared constraint hold up named work and records every
      attempt to get round it, and lets anyone dispute any record. It is neutral
      to the constraint and to the kind of entity that holds it. DAX is one
      constraint built on it.
    </p>
    <figure class="fig">
      <img src="/figures/constraint-protocol.svg" width="1000" height="640" alt="The Constraint Protocol in one picture: declare what you will not do, act, witness every event onto a signed record nobody can quietly edit, judge separately; attempts to get round a constraint are refused and recorded; anyone can dispute any record." loading="lazy" />
    </figure>
    <p class="note-line">A public working draft, run in full once so far.</p>
    <p class="out">
      <a href="https://github.com/daxfoundation/constraint-protocol">Read the specification &mdash; CP-SPEC v0.5, on GitHub</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
      <a href="https://jeyanandan.com/blog/the-constraint/">Why it matters: The Constraint</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="initiatives">
    <h2 id="initiatives">Initiatives</h2>
    <ul class="rows">
      <li class="row" id="learning">
        <span class="name">Meta DAX</span>
        <div class="text">
          <p>Our initiative of universal learning.</p>
          <p class="big">Every human being. Any subject. Any domain. Free, forever.</p>
          <p>Held to a benchmark with the audacity built in: <em>take a person who can communicate, and get them to the equivalent of a master&rsquo;s degree from an Ivy League university &mdash; on five minutes of connectivity a day.</em> <strong>We are developing this for all bandwidths!</strong> Five minutes a day is where it starts.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="fig span">
          <img src="/figures/meta-dax-tree.svg" width="1200" height="840" alt="Learning drawn as the tree of life upside down: every path from the first question is kept, the dead ends are kept, one learner's question answers another's, and the negative space nobody has explored yet is marked. There is never the way. There is always a way." loading="lazy" />
        </figure>
        <div class="text span">
          <p>Fourteen small experiments first. Two have run, with a model playing the teacher and a simulated adult as the learner. Then the legwork nobody writes a post about: identifiers and schemas that hold at a million nodes, reuse across languages without copying, the five-minute bundle, prompts certified by evals rather than by reputation. Then the twelve we can already picture: a tutor&rsquo;s move made once in Indonesian and offered in Spanish to a stranger who shares nothing with the first learner but kites; a course that grows from many teachers and many learners at once; one person on a phone going from five minutes a day to a master&rsquo;s. The small ones are how we earn the big ones.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="fig span">
          <img src="/figures/meta-dax-bridge.svg" width="1200" height="590" alt="The bridge: on the left, what has run, one teacher run and one learner run with a model in both chairs; across the span, the legwork, identifiers and schemas at scale, a registry of millions, reuse across languages, the five-minute bundle, certification, the constraint fields made live, the learner's privacy; on the right, the twelve experiments we can already picture, ending at five minutes to master's. The small ones earn the big ones." loading="lazy" />
        </figure>
        <div class="text span">
          <p>The measure under every one of them is <a href="/definitions/#human-delta-value">human delta value</a>: what the teacher brings, and what the teacher and their cognitive companion reach together that neither would alone. Meta DAX is built to invert the ratio of students to teachers, and to hand teachers back the cognition the busywork took, on purpose, for the problems they could previously only dream about. It is not a one-off measurement. It is the thread through all of it, and it is close to the point. <a href="/writing/human-delta-value/">Why, and how it is measured &rarr;</a></p>
        </div>
        <p class="go span"><a href="https://github.com/daxfoundation/metadax">Everything about Meta DAX is in the open: the benchmark, the experiments, every result, and where it is going &rarr;</a></p>
        <div class="note span" id="founder">
          <p class="note-head">A note from the founder</p>
          <p>I have been at this since I was 23. I am now 49.</p>
          <p>&ldquo;This&rdquo; has only ever been one thing: <a href="https://jeyanandan.com/blog/meta-dax/#what-i-am-fighting-for">the right information, to the right person, at the right time</a>. I found it the day I left university for the last time, and everything I have built since has been a draft of it. Meta DAX is the current one: the latest expansion of the same purpose, and the first where the information is the kind that lets a person go and get all the rest.</p>
          <p>It has taken a tremendous amount of personal sacrifice, on every level, and it still does. All of it has come out of my own pocket.</p>
          <p>If you want to know why it means this much to me, <a href="https://jeyanandan.com/blog/meta-dax/#what-meta-dax-means-to-me">it is here</a>. I was <a href="https://jeyanandan.com/blog/meta-dax/#the-kid-it-did-not-exist-for">the kid it did not exist for</a>.</p>
          <p>On the way to getting Meta DAX out, while working out how knowledge and intelligence could be made to compound, it became clear that I needed something else first: an accountability framework. A flight recorder, the black box the aviation industry keeps, for the thing I could see coming. I set that out in <a href="https://jeyanandan.com/blog/the-constraint/">The Constraint</a>. They are not two separate fights. The rubbish on the beach is a real problem, and so is the thousand-foot wave coming towards it, and you do not get to pick one. But while that work was in front of me I did not have the cognitive capacity to give Meta DAX what it needed, and I put it off from the beginning of this year. It is my purpose, and as of 28 September <a href="https://jeyanandan.com/blog/meta-dax/">it is in the open</a>: the experiments, every result, and what comes next.</p>
          <p>So, being completely transparent: if you would like to <a href="https://ko-fi.com/jeyanandan">buy me a coffee</a>, it would mean a great deal to me, from the bottom of my heart. Thank you. It goes to me personally, not the Foundation and not the company.</p>
          <p class="sig">&mdash; <a href="https://jeyanandan.com">Jason Jeyanandan</a></p>
        </div>
      </li>
      {initiatives.map((i) => (
        <li class="row">
          <span class="name">{i.name}</span>
          {i.status
            ? <span class="status">{i.status}</span>
            : <span class="text">{i.text}</span>}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="definitions-link">
    <h2 id="definitions-link">Definitions</h2>
    <p class="body">
      The normative definitions used across the DAX ecosystem &mdash; cognitive
      companion, human delta value, constraint, knowledge, intelligence, wisdom,
      consciousness and the rest &mdash; have their own page. Other documents
      reference them rather than restating them.
    </p>
    <p class="out"><a href="/definitions/">Read the definitions &rarr;</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-labelledby="reading">
    <h2 id="reading">Reading</h2>
    <p class="body">
      The long-form work behind all of this. Written by Jason Jeyanandan, who
      set this Foundation up, and published on his own site rather than here.
    </p>

    <ul class="rows">
      <li class="row">
        <span class="name"><a href="https://jeyanandan.com/blog/snowflake-fuckari-in-action/">The Snowflake</a></span>
        <span class="text">
          The mechanism. Constraint resonance run rather than summarised:
          constraint, process and valuation built up step by step on a single
          object.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li class="row">
        <span class="name"><a href="https://jeyanandan.com/blog/the-constraint/">The Constraint</a></span>
        <span class="text">
          The threat. Twenty-eight points in human history where meaning was
          assigned to people by something that was not those people, and why the
          speed matters more than the mechanism.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li class="row">
        <span class="name"><a href="https://jeyanandan.com/blog/fuckarian/">The Fuckarian</a></span>
        <span class="text">
          The person. Who built the framework, the loop and the protocol, why he
          has always been told to listen, what he thinks responsibility is, and
          who it is owed to.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li class="row">
        <span class="name"><a href="https://jeyanandan.com/blog/meta-dax/">Meta DAX: An Introduction</a></span>
        <span class="text">
          The point. What universal learning means to the person who set it in
          motion, what it has the nerve to attempt, and why it compounds.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li class="row">
        <span class="name"><a href="https://github.com/ObsidianDelta/Fuckery">Fuckery</a></span>
        <span class="text">
          The framework itself, published in full with its dead ends left in.
          The source of the emergence and constraint-resonance definitions.
        </span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  {/* Old links such as daxfoundation.org/#cognitive-companion still land:
      a definition anchor that no longer exists here forwards to the same
      anchor on /definitions/. */}
  <script is:inline define:vars={{ moved }}>
    (function () {
      var id = decodeURIComponent(location.hash.slice(1));
      if (id && moved.indexOf(id) !== -1) {
        location.replace('/definitions/' + (id === 'definitions' ? '' : '#' + id));
      }
    })();
  </script>
</Base>

<style>
  .lede { padding-block: 5rem 4.5rem; }
  h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 1.2rem + 2.6vw, 2.75rem);
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: 1.22;
    letter-spacing: -0.012em;
    max-width: 22em;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .block { padding-block: 2.25rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  h2 {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .rows { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  .row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 10rem 1fr;
    gap: 0.25rem 1.5rem;
    align-items: baseline;
    padding-block: 0.9rem;
  }
  .row + .row { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .name { font-weight: 500; }
  .text { color: var(--fg); }
  .text p { margin: 0; }
  .text p + p { margin-top: 0.75rem; }
  .status {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--faint);
  }
  .body { margin: 0; max-width: 36em; color: #cfcfcf; }

  /* One line that carries the weight of a section. */
  .big {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 1rem + 1.2vw, 1.6rem);
    line-height: 1.3;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    color: var(--fg);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .text .big { margin-bottom: 0; }
  strong { font-weight: 600; color: var(--fg); }

  /* The featured section: the Constraint Protocol. */
  .feature { padding-block: 3rem 2.75rem; }
  .note-line { margin: 1rem 0 0; font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--faint); }

  /* Figures: drawn in this palette, full width of the column. */
  .fig { margin: 1.75rem 0 0; }
  .fig img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 10px;
  }
  /* Inside a row, a figure or a link line spans both columns. */
  .row > .span { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
  .row > .fig { margin-top: 1.25rem; }
  .go { margin: 1rem 0 0.25rem; font-size: 0.9375rem; }

  /* Outbound links sit below the block they belong to, in a quiet register.
     Underlined rather than coloured: this palette has no accent. */
  .out { margin: 1.5rem 0 0; font-size: 0.9375rem; }
  .out span { color: var(--faint); margin-inline: 0.5rem; }
  .out a, .rows a, .go a, .note a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--faint);
  }
  .out a:hover, .out a:focus-visible,
  .rows a:hover, .rows a:focus-visible,
  .go a:hover, .go a:focus-visible,
  .note a:hover, .note a:focus-visible { border-bottom-color: currentColor; }
  .row[id] { scroll-margin-top: 1.5rem; }

  /* #founder: Jason's own words, set as a short letter inside the Meta DAX
     row, under the repository link. */
  .note { max-width: 36em; color: #cfcfcf; margin-top: 1.5rem; padding-top: 1.25rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .note p { margin: 0; }
  .note p + p { margin-top: 0.9rem; }
  .note .note-head {
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .note .sig { color: var(--muted); }
  .note .sig a { border-bottom-color: var(--line); }
  .text.span { margin-top: 1.25rem; }
  .text.span a { color: inherit; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--faint); }
  .text.span a:hover, .text.span a:focus-visible { border-bottom-color: currentColor; }

  @media (max-width: 34rem) {
    .fig { overflow-x: auto; -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch; }
    .fig img { width: 640px; max-width: none; }
    .lede { padding-block: 3rem 3rem; }
    .row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
